# Container Registry `${registry_name}`

A STACKIT container registry is ready in project `${stackit_project_id}`.

| Field | Value |
|-------|-------|
| **Registry** | `${registry_url}` |
| **Host** | `${registry_host}` |
| **Pull path** | `${registry_host}/${registry_name}/<image>:<tag>` |

## Who can open it

%{ if length(user_assignments) == 0 ~}
This project has no meshStack users yet, so only the platform's service account can reach the
registry. Add users to the project and re-run this building block.
%{ else ~}
Every meshStack user on this project holds a STACKIT registry role, which is what makes the Harbor
project show up for them. Sign in at `${registry_host}` with your STACKIT account — the Harbor
account is created on first sign-in.

| User | Registry role |
|------|---------------|
%{ for assignment in user_assignments ~}
| `${assignment.subject}` | `${assignment.role}` |
%{ endfor ~}
%{ endif ~}

## One manual step, once per registry

STACKIT grants no IAM role that opens the Harbor API to an identity Harbor does not know. Access
comes from linking a robot account to a STACKIT service account, and only the portal can create
that first link.

1. Open the registry in the STACKIT portal.
2. Create a robot account with **push**, **pull** and **robot account management** permissions.
3. Link it to the STACKIT service account the platform deploys as.

From then on that service account's access token authenticates as the robot, so every later robot
can be created through the Harbor API without touching the portal again.
